      {
        "caption_text": "We adapt this ﬁgure from the Focal Loss paper [9]. YOLOv3 runs signiﬁcantly faster than other detection methods with comparable performance. Times from either an M40 or Titan X, they are basically the same GPU.",
        "caption_type": "figure",
        "figure_id": "Figure 1",
        "line_number": 91
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "Bounding boxes with dimension priors and location prediction. We predict the width and height of the box as offsets from cluster centroids. We predict the center coordinates of the box relative to the location of ﬁlter application using a sigmoid function. This ﬁgure blatantly self-plagiarized from [15].",
        "caption_type": "figure",
        "figure_id": "Figure 2",
        "line_number": 151
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "Darknet-53.",
        "caption_type": "table",
        "figure_id": "Table 1",
        "line_number": 241
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "Comparison of backbones. Accuracy, billions of operations, billion ﬂoating point operations per second, and FPS for various networks.",
        "caption_type": "table",
        "figure_id": "Table 2",
        "line_number": 253
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "In terms of COCOs weird average mean AP metric it is on par with the SSD variants but is 3× faster. It is still quite a bit behind other",
        "caption_type": "table",
        "figure_id": "Table 3",
        "line_number": 266
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "I’m seriously just stealing all these tables from [9] they take soooo long to make from scratch. Ok, YOLOv3 is doing alright. Keep in mind that RetinaNet has like 3.8× longer to process an image. YOLOv3 is much better than SSD variants and comparable to state-of-the-art models on the AP50 metric.",
        "caption_type": "table",
        "figure_id": "Table 3",
        "line_number": 274
      },
      {
        "caption_text": "Again adapted from the [9], this time displaying speed/accuracy tradeoff on the mAP at .5 IOU metric. You can tell YOLOv3 is good because it’s very high and far to the left. Can you cite your own paper? Guess who’s going to try, this guy → [16]. Oh, I forgot, we also ﬁx a data loading bug in YOLOv2, that helped by like 2 mAP. Just sneaking this in here to not throw off layout.",
        "caption_type": "figure",
        "figure_id": "Figure 3",
        "line_number": 351
